NAME
       sgdisk - change GPT partition tables from the command line

SYNOPSIS
       sgdisk [OPTIONS] DISK

DESCRIPTION
       sgdisk is the scriptable sibling of gdisk: each change is an option on
       the command line, and sgdisk writes the table when it is done. It
       works on GPT disks (an MBR disk is converted). It is part of the gdisk
       package.

OPTIONS
       -p, --print
              Print the partition table.

       -n, --new NUM:START:END
              Create partition NUM. START and END can be sectors or sizes such
              as +1G; 0 means the default (first free sector, or the end of
              the free space).

       -t, --typecode NUM:CODE
              Set the type code: 8300 Linux, 8200 Linux swap, 8e00 Linux LVM,
              ef00 EFI system.

       -c, --change-name NUM:NAME
              Set the partition's name.

       -d, --delete NUM
              Delete partition NUM.

       -o, --clear
              Write a new, empty GPT (all partitions are lost).

       -Z, --zap-all
              Destroy both GPT and MBR data structures.

EXAMPLES
       A new 1 GiB LVM partition on /dev/vdb:

           sgdisk -n 1:0:+1G -t 1:8e00 /dev/vdb

NOTES
       Cert Lab does not simulate sgdisk: use gdisk, fdisk or parted there.

SEE ALSO
       gdisk(8), fdisk(8), parted(8), partprobe(8)
